[06/21/2026] jz@vesvault.com:
    libVES.c 1.35 / ves 1.09 - Post-quantum by default. liboqs is now required
    (configure fails without it; build --without-oqs to opt out), and newly
    generated vault keys default to ML-KEM-768 (FIPS 203) instead of ECDH.
    The default OQS parameter set moved from Kyber768 to ML-KEM-768 (Kyber768
    was removed in libOQS 0.12). Classical-only builds default to ECDH.
    Existing keys keep their own algorithm.

[08/04/2026] jz@vesvault.com:
    libVES.c 1.36 / ves 1.10 - Docker images are built and published from CI
    as vesvault/ves (the ves CLI) and vesvault/ves:dev (toolchain, headers
    and pkg-config, for building against libVES). The image now builds liboqs
    from a pinned source release; it used to copy it out of an abandoned 2023
    image carrying a liboqs too old for ML-KEM, so the published image had no
    working post-quantum path at all. configure --with-oqs-algo=ALGO selects
    the KEM used for newly generated vault keys and verifies that the
    installed liboqs provides it, at configure time and again at compile
    time, instead of failing at every key generation.

[10/04/2026] jz@vesvault.com:
    libVES.c 1.37 / ves 1.11 - Offline mode in the ves CLI: stream and item
    encryption with a key given on the command line, with no Vault and no
    network. -Y / -y supply a PEM public / private key (-G names the public
    key's algorithm, OQS by default), -I carries an item ciphertext, and -g
    generates a key pair. Offline stream encryption writes the cipher key in
    a header ahead of the ciphertext, encrypted for one or more recipients
    and authenticated by an HMAC, so one file decrypts with -d -y KEY, also
    through pipes. Fixes four bugs in ves 1.10: AES256GCM1K stream
    decryption overflowed a heap buffer on streams longer than about 32K
    (AES256GCMp likewise); a -p print followed by another option's f wrote
    the print into that option's file, truncating it; -P FILE and -C FILE
    (also --plaintext= / --ciphertext=) were refused as the help has them,
    only -Pf / -Cf worked; and on Windows the
    CLI read its input and wrote streams, item values and VESkeys in text
    mode, so binary data was cut short at the first 0x1A byte and had its
    line endings rewritten. Text output (PEM keys, JSON, listings) keeps
    Windows line endings. In the library, the JSON parser no longer reads
    past the end of its input when it converts a number.

[11/05/2026] jz@vesvault.com:
    libVES.c 1.38 / ves 1.11 - Fixes a key format bug: a post-quantum key of
    any OQS algorithm other than ML-KEM could not be read back when its public
    key ended in a zero byte, whether in a private key PEM or a stored vault
    key. The writer dropped those bytes from the BIT STRING, and the reader
    refused the result with "Unsupported PEM private key". That affects about
    one sntrup761 key in four and one Kyber768 key in 256. ML-KEM, the default,
    has its own writer and was never affected. The writer now keeps the bytes,
    and the reader restores them, so keys already written open again, with no
    action needed; 1.37 can read keys 1.38 writes. Also, a build that does not
    run configure, such as Android's ndk-build, no longer fails on config.h.
